## Why This Is Asked

Testing fundamental low-level synchronization concepts, including memory ordering and fairness, with a simple, traceable pattern suitable for beginners but illustrative of real-world constraints.

## Key Concepts

- Atomic operations and per-thread state
- Memory order: relaxed, acquire, release on x86-64
- Fairness and progress guarantees in multi-threaded code
- Busy-wait implications and micro-benchmarks

## Code Example

```c
#include <stdatomic.h>
#include <stdint.h>

typedef struct {
  _Atomic uint32_t next_ticket;
  _Atomic uint32_t now_serving;
} ticket_lock_t;

static _Thread_local uint32_t my_ticket;

void ticket_lock(ticket_lock_t* lk) {
  my_ticket = atomic_fetch_add_explicit(&lk->next_ticket, 1, memory_order_relaxed);
  while (atomic_load_explicit(&lk->now_serving, memory_order_acquire) != my_ticket) {
    // pause/busy-wait
  }
}

void ticket_unlock(ticket_lock_t* lk) {
  atomic_store_explicit(&lk->now_serving, my_ticket + 1, memory_order_release);
}

int ticket_try_lock(ticket_lock_t* lk) {
  // Non-blocking path is optional for this simple ticket lock; not implemented here
  return -1;
}
